**Convention (Slice 1: census and skeleton)** · `conv:buildgraph:slice-one` Declare the root and directory lists, the full step roster and report edges, external-tool prerequisites, explicit exclusion reasons and a fresh census target. Keep existing commands on their current route while checking every tracked file and each roster name against the graph. Add negative witnesses for a new tracked source, a new tracked dotfile, an unconnected declared input, a stale path, a widened exclusion and a missing step, plus positive vendor and generated-file cases. Verify a new file is detected without reconfiguration. Gate the skeleton and its claim evidence before delegation; the additional accounting check strengthens coverage without changing any existing command's predicate.

**Convention (Slice 2: policy and documents)** · `conv:buildgraph:slice-two` Delegate policy, registry, checker-witness, corpus, correspondence and document targets with their helpers and declared inputs. Preserve the fixed-point write/clean order and repeated read-only checks. On the same source and configuration compare legacy and graph results for clean, stale-render and deliberately failing-check fixtures in isolated checkouts; require the same command statuses, diagnostics classes and unchanged committed bytes. Prove a failed producer or missing report cannot yield a green consumer. Keep unmoved commands in the entry until their own slice passes.

**Convention (Slice 3: Cargo and measurements)** · `conv:buildgraph:slice-three` Delegate formatting, Clippy, the advisory nursery, feature-minimal and documentation builds, configuration measurements, benchmark work and observation build targets. Preserve exact Cargo arguments, feature sets, target/profile selection and the separate doctest path; serialize access to the same Cargo target directory. Compare the artifact and test inventories and exercise an injected compile failure, an advisory finding and a timing excess. No newly enabled optimization, dependency, feature or compiler change is bundled with the composition move.

**Convention (Slice 4: runtime evidence)** · `conv:buildgraph:slice-four` Delegate test execution, live-service lifecycle, observation/runtime conformance, budget reporting, harness matrix and claim evidence after their producer targets. Preserve all cases and current-source/configuration joins, held evidence and sibling failure propagation. Exercise service startup refusal, interrupted cleanup, a failing test, a stale case result and unavailable measurements. Verify port ownership and server shutdown under concurrent gates without introducing parallel execution inside this graph. Require the same semantic outcomes as the existing entry before removing its corresponding calls.

**Convention (Slice 5: entry completion)** · `conv:buildgraph:slice-five` Remove the remaining duplicated sequencing from the entry after every step and final report consumer has moved. Compare the complete roster, feature configurations, case inventory, document bytes, claim matrix and final statuses across fresh and reused build directories. Require every named step to run exactly once, unchanged trees to retain fresh evidence, and a missing graph target or report to fail. Retain the external entry and service cleanup, publish the declared/excluded census and whole-gate measurements, and record measured costs without promising a speedup. Each preceding slice is a separately gated change and preserves the existing verdicts; the green documentation gate for this record implements none of them.

**Gate (Composition evidence)** · `gate:buildgraph:implementation` Owner: the build-graph implementer with independent gate-contract review. Admitted under (`amend:buildgraph:implementation-admission`) on the slices' test-site claims for complete file and step accounting, source/configuration/run freshness, actual-status propagation, unchanged command and case inventories, generator fixed points, cleanup, and nonverdict timing. Each slice attaches its scoped evidence under (`rule:testing:coverage`) and (`rule:testing:results`); absence of a claim remains a named hold, not proof from the graph. Admit the completed migration only with the equivalence and negative witnesses in (`conv:buildgraph:slice-one`), (`conv:buildgraph:slice-two`), (`conv:buildgraph:slice-three`), (`conv:buildgraph:slice-four`) and (`conv:buildgraph:slice-five`).


**Amendment (Census skeleton)** · `amend:buildgraph:census-skeleton` Repository amendment: the first slice of (`dec:buildgraph:composition`) and (`gate:buildgraph:implementation`) adds the root Meson graph, explicit directory input lists, the shell-step roster with serial report predecessors and final report consumers, and one fresh census step. Existing shell commands and predicates keep their route; the graph does not execute their skeleton nodes. Meson evaluates the lists into a build-directory manifest; the always-run census target reconciles that manifest with the live Git index, the shell roster and the deployed linter census on each invocation. No tracked path is excluded and the exclusion pattern matches nothing; changing either policy is refused. Declared consumers are conservative input envelopes, not proof that every command reads every listed byte. The registered tracked-corpus witness (`claim:discipline:build-graph-census-accounts-for-tracked-inputs`) exercises new tracked source and dotfile additions without reconfiguration, unconnected and stale declarations, widened exclusions, missing steps, duplicate declarations and report-edge defects, with positive vendor and generated-file cases and a fresh repeat. This evidence narrows the hold to step delegation, run/result adapters, complete artifact joins and command-equivalence slices; it does not admit the completed migration.

**Convention (Adding a tracked input)** · `conv:buildgraph:adding-files` Add a tracked file to its directory's meson.build local_inputs list in the same change, naming its source-root path, consuming step names and its generator step when applicable. Every non-root directory has its list mirrored under ci/build-inputs, so typed input and template directories, crate sources and existing package build definitions remain untouched; the root list is meson.build. The refusal names both the undeclared file and the precise list to edit; a new directory also adds its subdir call to the enclosing list. Every new Meson list declares itself with the build-graph consumer. Stage the file and list together, then run the unchanged bash ci/gate.sh entry. It refuses an unset or empty PN_MESON_BIN; the caller supplies the provisioned Meson executable, while Ninja is found on PATH and the census uses LINTER_BIN. A newly tracked file is checked even when Meson has not reconfigured, because the census reads the current index rather than a configuration-time file discovery. Untracked run products remain under .lane; generated tracked files, vendor files and tracked dotfiles receive explicit declarations rather than directory exemptions.

**Amendment (Policy and document delegation)** · `amend:buildgraph:policy-delegation` Repository amendment: slice 2 of (`dec:buildgraph:composition`) and (`gate:buildgraph:implementation`) delegates the thirty assigned policy, registry, checker, corpus and document steps to separate always-run Meson targets. The twenty Cargo and runtime steps assigned to slices 3 and 4 retain their shell route; build-graph moves to slice 5 because it already invokes Meson and cannot safely invoke that same build recursively. The entry retains the serial command inventory, service cleanup, per-step wall and resource deltas, diagnostic extraction and final aggregation. A target executes the exact roster command in the source root and publishes its raw status and merged output, UTC bounds and command wall; successful report publication is not command success. The entry reads that current report and returns the raw command status through its existing reporter. Inclusive step walls include composition overhead; the target report separates the command wall. The controlled legacy route selects the same commands for equivalence measurements, never a second execution in the same gate.

**Rule (Delegated report boundary)** · `rule:buildgraph:delegated-reports` The migrated targets use the complete explicitly declared tracked set as a conservative input envelope, including their runner and list helpers; no recursive file discovery replaces the census. Each request binds its unique invocation, source commit, declared input bytes, public execution configuration, configured graph, canonically ordered completed dependency reports and fresh request identity. The adapter rechecks that binding immediately before execution, removes the old target result, atomically publishes the new report and checks its output digest before accepting it. Missing, malformed, stale or mismatched reports refuse structurally. Dependency edges require current completion; they do not silently turn every earlier checker into a success prerequisite, because the existing gate deliberately continues independent checks after refusal. A recorded failed command remains failed in final aggregation. Graph configuration remains a prerequisite for delegated steps: a missing slice assignment, failed setup or failed regeneration structurally refuses every delegated step with status 2, including delegated witnesses; these are absent command verdicts, not independent command failures. The shell steps still run. Running commands outside that refused graph would bypass its source and configuration binding. The configuration fixtures exercise all three refusals, separately from the continued execution after an ordinary command refusal. Direct Meson invocation without a current gate request refuses. Final report joins and interruption cleanup are checked under (`amend:buildgraph:entry-completion`); conservative input accounting still does not prove complete semantic dependencies.

**Amendment (Scoped delegation evidence)** · `amend:buildgraph:delegation-evidence` Repository amendment: for (`dec:buildgraph:composition`), (`claim:discipline:build-graph-census-accounts-for-tracked-inputs`) continues to evidence file and roster accounting. (`claim:discipline:build-graph-delegation-preserves-results`) compares clean, stale-render and failing-check sequences in isolated Git checkouts through the actual Meson adapter: command statuses, merged diagnostics, generated-byte differences and repeated reads agree, a status-seven refusal survives, and changed source, configuration, run, input or result envelopes refuse. These are constructed adapter witnesses, not proof of every checker's semantic dependencies. The gate retains (`gate:buildgraph:implementation`) as held for the remaining Cargo/runtime delegation, complete artifact joins, cleanup and final entry comparison; the scoped evidence narrows that hold without claiming the completed migration.

**Amendment (Delegation overhead)** · `amend:buildgraph:delegation-overhead` Repository amendment: invoke the Meson-generated always-run targets through Ninja directly, avoiding a separate Meson introspection load for each step. Ninja retains Meson's regeneration edge: changed configuration regenerates before execution, unchanged configuration is reused, and a missing build directory still requires setup. Every tracked-input digest, predecessor report and fresh request check in (`rule:buildgraph:delegated-reports`) remains; reading the slice assignment once for predecessor traversal removes repeated work without caching a verdict. Target reports separate command user and system CPU from the inclusive entry measurement, while per-request measurements record preparation and driver costs. Each delegated STEP line also prints command_wall_s and delegation_overhead_s, the inclusive wall less the command wall; unavailable command evidence is marked unavailable rather than borrowed from another run. The command log itself remains unchanged. (`claim:discipline:build-graph-census-accounts-for-tracked-inputs`) checks configuration reuse and regeneration as well as fresh census execution, and (`claim:discipline:build-graph-delegation-preserves-results`) retains the status, diagnostic, byte and freshness comparisons through the generated Ninja targets. The observation-syntax assignment explicitly splits from the observation-runtime group: its cargo run --profile gate example remains delegated in slice 2, while service-backed runtime evidence remains in slice 4. (`claim:discipline:build-graph-slices-cover-roster`) refuses an unexplained group split.

**Amendment (Cargo delegation)** · `amend:buildgraph:cargo-delegation` Repository amendment: for (`dec:buildgraph:composition`), (`claim:discipline:build-graph-census-accounts-for-tracked-inputs`) retains file and roster accounting, and (`claim:discipline:build-graph-delegation-preserves-results`) retains adapter status, diagnostic and freshness comparisons. Slice 3 of (`conv:buildgraph:slice-three`) routes cargo-config-measurement, fmt, clippy, nursery-advisory, minimal, rustdoc, benchmarks, observation-build and host-observation-build through the same always-run target adapter as slice 2. All nine command texts, Cargo arguments, feature selections, profiles and target selection remain unchanged. The serial shell entry waits for each target report before invoking the next, retaining one Cargo target directory and no parallel Cargo execution. The tests step and its separate doctest path remain on their existing route. (`claim:discipline:cargo-delegation-preserves-build-evidence`) compares constructed Cargo artifact and test inventories, including a separate doctest command, an injected compiler error, an advisory warning and an injected timing excess; the excess remains a finding with no verdict effect. These scoped witnesses attach to (`gate:buildgraph:implementation`) without admitting the remaining runtime delegation, artifact joins or final entry migration. Thirty-nine steps are delegated; the eleven slice-4 runtime steps and the slice-5 build-graph entry remain on the shell route.

**Amendment (Configuring Meson version)** · `amend:buildgraph:meson-version` Repository amendment: once at the start of each gate, compare the version returned by PN_MESON_BIN with meson_version.full in the build directory's meson-info/meson-info.json. Reuse matching configuration; replace a mismatched configuration through setup --wipe using the answering binary, and verify the resulting recorded version before invoking Ninja. A missing build directory receives ordinary setup. This prevents Ninja's retained regeneration command from silently keeping an earlier configuring version when the selected tool changes. Failed version inspection or setup is a structural refusal under (`rule:buildgraph:delegated-reports`). (`claim:discipline:build-graph-census-accounts-for-tracked-inputs`) witnesses matching-version reuse and mismatched-version setup; per-target freshness and always-run census checks remain unchanged.

