**Amendment (Checked admission retention)** · `amend:buildgraph:checked-retention` Repository amendment: (`claim:discipline:retained-gates-preserve-complete-evidence`) exercises capture itself on a constructed tracked checkout with actual retained artifacts. A dirty tree, reordered or repeated roster, changed configured command, duplicate exit marker, mismatched status, missing final join, stale report source/run/configuration/graph/history/step/command/status, altered report output, duplicated or stale case, stale claim or matrix source, and multiple or invalid Meson actions each refuse; the clean fixture retains and remains unchanged by those refusals. Comparison in checked admission mode requires service execution requested for admission in both snapshots, even when two ordinary held snapshots are otherwise equal. (`claim:discipline:service-selection-keeps-unexecuted-evidence-held`) checks configured-tool fingerprint binding and captures its synthetic runner output internally. The real runner prints one flushed BUILD-SERVICE-SELECTION line with source commit and the actual admission flag; successful service events retain their selection reason, flag and fingerprint so the comparison can check them. These strengthen the ongoing evidence procedure of (`amend:buildgraph:entry-completion`) without retroactively changing the verbatim historical admission line.


**Amendment (Bounded concurrent invocations)** · `amend:buildgraph:bounded-concurrency` Independent commands share the single Meson roster and its explicit acyclic completion dependencies. The scheduler admits at most {{ row('org.peer-network.harness.registry', kind='tier', name='fast').jobs | number }} command processes at once. This replaces the serial entry policy in (`amend:buildgraph:entry-completion`), (`amend:buildgraph:cargo-delegation`) and (`amend:buildgraph:runtime-delegation`): shared-target Cargo work remains serialized, while prebuilt test invocations and independent checkers may overlap. Fixed-point generation and its clean-tree check precede concurrent readers. Wallet conformance deliberately mutates sources for refusal and compile-failure witnesses, so its required invocation owns an isolated checkout and a separate retained Cargo target beneath the caller's target root. Those probes cannot race a concurrent source reader or compiler; their original assertions and diagnostics remain unchanged. Current-run products require their producer's completion before any consumer starts. Ordinary failures remain in the aggregate while independent checks continue; shutdown remains unconditional.

Meson still configures each target and the complete input envelope. Concurrent requests invoke that configured target's existing executor directly, avoiding simultaneous Ninja drivers writing the same build directory; the controlled serial route retains Ninja dispatch. Both routes bind the same command, current source and configuration, complete input digest, fresh nonce and dependency history, and validate the same atomic result and output digest. Finalization requires every roster member exactly once, validates dependency order and every current result, and accepts no cached verdict. (`claim:discipline:build-graph-completion-is-current-and-exact`) retains its serial refusal witnesses and adds independent completion, missing-dependency and duplicate-history controls.

The test build publishes one complete inventory. Disjoint fast, reference-certificate and real-service nextest invocations consume that inventory with retries disabled; the fast invocation retains doctests. The evidence join refuses a missing, duplicate, stale, ignored or incomplete required case before the matrix and claim evaluator run. (`rule:design:development-budgets`) governs the per-commit wall and the separately required tier budgets. Each step and tier reports its own process CPU and elapsed wall. Concurrent cgroup samples describe the gate's sampling interval and retain their gate-owned or shared-node scope; overlapping deltas are neither attributed to individual processes nor added together. Tier CPU sums only its own step processes. Final evidence-join time is reported separately, and import requires the entire gate green.


Every failed required case enters a durable flake ledger under the caller's retained target directory, with commit, case, public error and node load. A later execution after that failure reports a retry and its outcome; a recurrence is a finding even when the later execution passes. Nextest still performs no automatic retries. Administrative wallet calls retain their public duration lines in the fixture root, and wallet-elements-stop publishes them on success as well as failure. A passing administrative call above the steady-state socket bound enters the same ledger, so administrative headroom cannot hide a stall. Private parameters and credentials are absent from these records.

**Amendment (Commit-scoped wallet evidence)** · `amend:buildgraph:wallet-commit-inputs` A concurrent checkout can change while a required invocation runs. Wallet conformance therefore takes its complete input from the source commit's Git objects and verifies a clean isolated checkout at that exact commit; the parent's working files are neither an input nor a stability condition. Its mutation witnesses and compiler use the isolated tree and its own retained Cargo target. The harness result, compile-refusal log and performance findings return through distinct untracked .lane paths owned by this step, with a complete temporary file atomically renamed over each destination. The performance sidecar is wallet-conformance-findings.jsonl; it does not append to another step's findings. The isolated invocation's flake ledger lives in its retained target. Existing producer-completion edges still govern consumers; atomic publication prevents a reader from observing a partial artifact. (`claim:harness:wallet-inputs-are-commit-scoped`) changes a tracked parent file during the invocation and retains the same result artifacts as the clean-parent run, while sibling findings remain unchanged.

**Amendment (Isolated host refusal evidence)** · `amend:buildgraph:host-commit-inputs` Host observation's compiler-refusal probes deliberately change source files. They run from a clean checkout of the source commit with their own Cargo target and PostgreSQL instance, so concurrent readers cannot compile a probe's temporary program. The harness result, compiler refusal and performance findings return by atomic replacement to step-owned untracked paths. (`claim:harness:host-inputs-are-commit-scoped`) exercises the isolation boundary while the parent changes. Other checks consume committed inputs after the fixed-point checks. Their mutation suites also enter through the common commit-scoped helper. The build graph orders declared build-output producers before their consumers.

**Amendment (Service restart ownership)** · `amend:buildgraph:postgresql-restart-port` A stopped TCP listener no longer owns its port. PostgreSQL fixture restart waits for shutdown, allocates an available port under the same mutex as initial startup, and launches once with the existing database. It atomically replaces the fixture endpoint files only after startup succeeds. A competing listener on the released port cannot redirect the restarted fixture; (`claim:discipline:storage-restart-preserves-committed-bytes`) retains that listener through restart and reads the original committed bytes without appending. A failed stop or launch remains a failure, without a retry. Every PostgreSQL fixture owns a private socket directory under the declared TMPDIR, independent of checkout depth; stop removes it only after the server releases it. Before server startup, the complete socket name must fit the platform byte limit, including space for its terminating byte; an overlong name refuses with its measured byte length. Unix and loopback TCP transport evidence are both retained. (`claim:discipline:postgresql-instances-own-distinct-ports`) exercises an overlong former placement, independent temporary directories, successful queries and cleanup.

**Convention (Child stream destinations)** · `conv:buildgraph:child-stream-destinations` Configuring a child to write a file, a pipe or the null sink does not emit to the parent's streams. The source checker admits explicit destination constructors throughout the tree and keeps their argument expressions visible to the printing checks. Unknown destinations fail closed; explicit inheritance and direct printing remain refused. A capture witness reads its file after child completion, so reader scheduling cannot limit capture progress.

**Rule (Writable witness trees)** · `rule:buildgraph:writable-witness-trees` No gate step writes tracked files of the shared gate root. The common isolation helper is the sole route to a writable source tree for generation checks and CI mutation suites. It checks out the source commit from Git objects and verifies its identity and clean tracked bytes before execution; synthetic negative fixtures are created inside that invocation. A shared-root digest is not a synchronization mechanism and is not used. Each invocation owns its artifacts and atomically publishes complete results to distinct untracked destinations. Read-only runtime consumers may use declared shared build products, but compilation and source mutation stay in the invocation's own tree and target. An explicit import base is resolved and validated in the parent as a commit ancestral to the source, then passed across isolation by immutable commit identity; a parent-local ref name cannot be an input inside the child. An absent or unrelated base refuses before execution. Mutable isolated trees live outside the gate checkout. PN_ISOLATION_ROOT may declare that directory explicitly and refuses a location inside the checkout; otherwise the helper uses the external Cargo target, or a sibling isolation directory when Cargo targets are inside the checkout. A checkout identity and invocation owner partition this storage so concurrent checkouts cannot share writable trees. The census witness pauses its reader after snapshot hand-off, creates and removes an actual isolated tree and an untracked live-root subtree, then completes a census whose counts equal the unmutated run. Persistent isolated clones own their objects without alternates, so parent garbage collection cannot strand their detached heads. Older shared trees are replaced while their separately keyed Cargo targets remain retained. A dependency-lock change does not select a new empty target: Cargo retains artifacts by package identity and compilation fingerprint, including dependency graph, features, compiler and build inputs, and rebuilds changed packages and their dependents. Lock entries alone are insufficient cache keys because equal package versions can compile under different feature or dependency selections. The outer target still separates toolchain and declared compilation environments; each checkout and invocation owner keeps its own target. No artifacts are copied between owners or manually marked fresh. This keeps unaffected third-party products without making stale workspace results reusable, and avoids accumulating a fresh target for every lock revision. Superseded configuration targets and the former in-checkout trees are removed under the owner lock; lock files remain stable. An owner unused across {{ row('org.peer-network.devperf.budgets', name='gate').number_forms.isolation_retention_imports | display }} distinct import-base trees loses its source tree and all retained target artifacts. Repeated gates on the same import do not age storage; active owners are skipped. Each use renews the owner generation. Expiration is per owner, leaving Cargo responsible for artifact validity within a retained target. Active owners also bound retained profile caches, including target-triple layouts, at each locked entry: the ceiling is {{ row('org.peer-network.devperf.budgets', name='gate').number_forms.owner_target_ceiling_bytes | display }} bytes per isolated owner and {{ row('org.peer-network.devperf.budgets', name='gate').number_forms.shared_target_ceiling_bytes | display }} bytes for the shared gate target at the serial preflight boundary. The gate takes an exclusive lock in its declared Cargo target before preflight and holds it through execution and cleanup, so another gate cannot sweep that target while it is compiling. A supervisor owns that lock and prints GATE-TARGET-LOCK with the measured wait; its descriptor is closed in the gate child and every descendant, so a detached service cannot extend the lock lifetime. Cargo root metadata distinguishes nested owner targets from target-triple profiles: a nested root has its own rustc information or cache-directory tag, while a triple contains profile fingerprints without a root marker. Nested targets and unrecognized directories are reported outside the shared profile ceiling, not swept as triples. Direct builds outside the gate must use a different target or honor the same lock. These are retention ceilings, not limits on a running compilation. When allocated cache bytes exceed the ceiling, remove incremental state first; if the remaining gate, debug, release and documentation profiles, native or under a target triple, still exceed it, remove those profiles for Cargo to rebuild. Preserve baseline records, isolation storage, locks and other owners. This bounds accumulated superseded artifacts while retaining dependency products when dropping incremental state suffices. TARGET-DIRS names the before and after allocations and ceiling; a swept isolated owner is partial for comparison. No cache is swept while its owner executes. TARGET-DIRS reports allocated bytes for each owner at gate completion and separately names directories outside the profile ceiling, including isolation storage and baseline metadata. These reported bytes are not silently included in the profile bound; isolated owners retain their own entry ceiling and idle-import retention. The isolation witnesses prune an otherwise unreachable parent commit, retain it in the child, and execute the next checkout successfully; retention witnesses keep active storage, remove obsolete keys and expire idle artifacts without sharing a target.

**Convention (Retained isolated builds)** · `conv:buildgraph:retained-isolated-builds` Isolated sources and Cargo targets live beneath the checkout's preserved target directory, partitioned by invocation owner. A lock prevents overlapping reuse of an owner. The source tree is refreshed to the requested commit without rewriting unchanged files; temporary fixtures and results are cleared on entry. Target keys bind the pinned toolchain, lockfile and compiler configuration. Cargo still validates source and compiler fingerprints on every invocation; the cache is a build optimization, never a stored test verdict. Changed commits run fresh tests and publish fresh evidence. A fixture proves unchanged source timestamps and retained cache bytes across calls, and a changed compiler configuration selects another target.

**Rule (Exclusive Cargo resource)** · `rule:buildgraph:exclusive-cargo` The Meson roster declares shared-target compiling steps as an exclusive resource class. Dependency validation requires every pair in each class to be ordered transitively, before scheduling begins. An unordered pair is a structural refusal; a Cargo lock wait is not a substitute for the graph contract. Owned isolated targets do not belong to the shared class.

**Convention (Independent delegation scenarios)** · `conv:buildgraph:independent-scenarios` The runtime delegation witness compares every legacy and Meson outcome, cleanup, refusal and evidence assertion. Independent scenarios own separate fixture trees and run under the harness declaration's {{ row('org.peer-network.harness.registry', kind='suite', name='ci::test_linter_entry').scenario_jobs | number }}-job bound; commands within each scenario retain their dependency order. All scenario results are joined before comparison, and the signal-cleanup witness runs afterwards. This removes serial waiting without omitting a linter invocation or weakening an assertion.

**Rule (Case calibration)** · `rule:buildgraph:case-calibration` Run `python3 -B ci/calibrate.py` separately from the concurrent gate. Use its --campaign mode for allowance sizing: a complete discarded warm-up precedes the declared warm samples under (`conv:design:case-budget-calibration`), all at the same committed source and configuration with the retained target. Each invocation and its resources remain in the campaign artifact; a failed invocation stops the campaign without replacement. It takes a committed source tree through the common isolation helper, builds its complete inventory, then runs each active suite alone. Nextest and doctest cases execute serially with retries disabled; contract cases retain their parent execution and benchmarks retain their declared sampling policy. Mutation witnesses own the calibration tree, which no other suite consumes during their run. Missing, duplicate, ignored or held case results refuse the calibration result union. Each suite records UTC bounds, own process CPU, node load and shared cgroup deltas; each nextest process and serial Python case records its own user and system CPU beside its wall. The nextest calibration runner excludes its own startup from the case wall, retains the outer runner wall separately, and joins the measured process to the exact source, case and outcome. CPU includes reaped descendants. Cases inside shared doctest, contract or benchmark parents explicitly report unavailable case CPU rather than borrowing the parent total. The calibration comparison names both source commits, their range, changed files and conservative package-code change flags; declaration changes remain separately visible; external node activity remains visible context, not suite CPU. Case budgets are judged here. The concurrent gate's BUDGET-LEDGER remains informational and cannot change a functional verdict. Calibration retains every over-budget case with its declared tier and implementation, without changing another package's budgets or assertions.
